import{_ as a}from"./chunks/recovery-ladder.FLX6d5JY.js";import{_ as r,o as t,c as n,ao as o}from"./chunks/framework.AUoJnklv.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Progression and evidence","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"progression-evidence.md","filePath":"progression-evidence.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"progression-evidence.md"};function s(l,e,d,c,h,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="Progression-and-evidence" tabindex="-1">Progression and evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#Progression-and-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Progression and evidence {#Progression-and-evidence}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This is an evidence history, not a release timeline. <code>HSquared.jl</code> is currently experimental <strong>0.8.0</strong>; the R-public <code>public_covered_count</code> is <strong>7</strong>. Neither fact means production readiness, Julia General registration, calibrated intervals, or a <strong>0.9</strong> release. For applied work, begin in <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/hsquared/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">hsquared</a>.</p><h2 id="Where-we-are-now" tabindex="-1">Where we are now <a class="header-anchor" href="#Where-we-are-now" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where we are now {#Where-we-are-now}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Read the <a href="./validation-status">live validation table</a> for route-specific scope. A Julia engine row can be covered while its R formula, payload mapping, or public workflow remains planned or partial. The <a href="./twin-boundary">twin boundary</a> explains that distinction before an applied claim is made.</p><p>Point estimates may be reported only inside the stated route scope. Intervals and standard errors do not have a shared coverage guarantee: inspect each route&#39;s claim boundary rather than borrowing evidence from a neighbour.</p><h2 id="How-a-route-earns-its-label" tabindex="-1">How a route earns its label <a class="header-anchor" href="#How-a-route-earns-its-label" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How a route earns its label {#How-a-route-earns-its-label}&quot;">​</a></h2><figure class="hs-figure"><img src="'+a+'" alt="Three-stage ladder: planned, partial, and covered. The figure states that internal agreement alone is not enough for covered status."><figcaption>A label is route-specific evidence, not a general production badge. The generated live table is the source of truth.</figcaption></figure><p>A route moves from <strong>planned</strong> to <strong>partial</strong> when a bounded implementation and its limits are recorded. <strong>Covered</strong> is a stronger, scoped evidence label: it does not widen the route, make it default, or settle interval calibration. The <a href="./validation-status">validation table</a> gives the current evidence and the <a href="./audience-comparators">audience/comparator page</a> explains why comparison must match the estimand.</p><h2 id="Historical-milestones,-each-with-a-boundary" tabindex="-1">Historical milestones, each with a boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#Historical-milestones,-each-with-a-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Historical milestones, each with a boundary {#Historical-milestones,-each-with-a-boundary}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>0.1 contract — June 2026 foundation.</strong> Pedigree normalization and sparse <code>Ainv</code> were established as engine utilities, while the narrow R formula contract still stopped honestly at the bridge boundary. This is the starting contract, not a production fitting claim; see the <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/design/01-v0.1-contract.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">0.1 contract</a> and its <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/dev-log/after-task/2026-06-13-phase-1a-pedigree-ainv.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">pedigree/Ainv record</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Early 0.2–0.4 development — June–July 2026.</strong> Two-effect, arbitrary-N independent-effect, k=2 random-regression, and direct–maternal work each earned separate, bounded evidence. These are development milestones rather than asserted release tags; none transfers its evidence to another model or to production scale. See the <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/dev-log/after-task/2026-06-13-two-effect-reml.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">two-effect record</a>, <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/dev-log/after-task/2026-07-01-phase2-r-arbitrary-1g.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arbitrary-N R-surface record</a>, <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/dev-log/recovery-checkpoints/2026-07-01-rr-k2-covered-evidence.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">k=2 random-regression evidence</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/HSquared.jl/blob/main/docs/dev-log/after-task/2026-07-02-phase4-direct-maternal.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">direct–maternal record</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>0.5.0 — later experimental numbering marker.</strong> The project moved its experimental numbering from <code>0.0.1</code> to <code>0.5.0</code> without claiming a production engine or Julia General registration. This does not rewrite the separate R-package release history, including its verified v0.1.0 GitHub release. See the <a href="/HSquared.jl/previews/PR320/changelog#0.5.0-experimental">0.5.0 changelog</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>0.6.0 — R-public multivariate milestone.</strong> The R-public count changed from 5 to 6, while the engine multivariate row was already covered. This is a public-surface milestone, not a new engine promotion or universal multivariate reporting permission. See the <a href="/HSquared.jl/previews/PR320/changelog#0.6.0-experimental;-historical">0.6.0 record</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>0.7.0 — genomic GREML milestone.</strong> The R-public count changed from 6 to 7. It does not turn every genomic route, interval, or sparse path into a covered default. See the <a href="/HSquared.jl/previews/PR320/changelog#0.7.0-experimental">0.7.0 changelog</a> and the live <a href="./validation-status">genomic rows</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>0.8.0 — engine FA and single-step pillars.</strong> The named engine cells are deliberately narrow: FA is S4 <code>t=4</code>, <code>K=1</code>, positive Ledermann slack, interior uniqueness, and rotation-invariant <code>G</code>/<code>R</code>/<code>ψ</code>; single-step is H-scale <code>σ²a/σ²e</code> under ordinary defaults and <code>G=A₂₂+0.05I</code>. R factor-analytic grammar remains planned and R single-step remains opt-in partial. See the <a href="/HSquared.jl/previews/PR320/changelog#0.8.0-experimental">0.8.0 changelog</a> and <a href="./validation-status">current status</a>.</p></li></ul><h2 id="What-remains-separate" tabindex="-1">What remains separate <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-remains-separate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What remains separate {#What-remains-separate}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Julia registry status, any future Julia-package release decision, interval calibration, production readiness, and future roadmaps are separate decisions. This page does not announce a release or change a status row. For technical plans, use the <a href="./roadmap">roadmap</a> and <a href="./backend-algorithm-roadmap">developer pages</a>; for an applied reporting decision, return to the <a href="./twin-boundary">twin boundary</a>.</p>',12)])])}const f=r(i,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
